cessary to govern the village and safely pass through the disaster.\" [1093] Iwakura maintained a non-intervention stance towards the source of faith. [1094] \"Indeed, so, for the villagers who seem to disbelieve the Kagiyama family and those who believe to the extreme, do you know anything?\" [1095] \"This is a topic that will never come up in idle chatter, right? The younger the person, the less real they should feel about it. Even in such a rural area, young people are still young people.\" [1096] A young person who has just come from a big city evaluates it. [1097] \"Hmm, as the date approaches, if the people around here become hysterical, it may induce fanaticism. I think, in the Kagetani family, Kanae-san is truly the heir, and she firmly believes in it. Because her intention of 'having to save the village' is clear. Her younger sisters and Hikari-san don't seem to be as convinced. And as for Sakura-san, it seems she wants to leave here.\" [1098] \"Is the sister of the Shizuki adult going to the outside world?\" [1099] Oyama also seems shocked. [1100] \"Because she is a woman with a strong curiosity. From the examples so far, women, especially the sister of the Shizuki adult, should stay here like the current Miyuki-san. Although I am talking about old-fashioned customs, the sister of the Shizuki adult has a less constrained outlook. Of course, the adults strongly oppose her.\" [1101] \"Ms. Yotsuka has a free spirit.\" [1102] Inheriting her mother's name, Oyuki's words are hard to grasp, whether they are mocking or envious. At least, Shizuma cannot tell. Can Iwakura tell? [1103] \"Because freedom is also unexpectedly troublesome.\" [1104] Iwakura only responds with a hesitant expression. [1105] \"Thank you, in all aspects\u2026\"\n[1106] Yuki stood up from the bench, smoothed her hair that was blowing in the wind, and left the terrace. [1107] * *\n[1108][1] The words of Iwakura come from Jane Austen's Pride and Prejudice, Chapter 58. [1109] 6\n[1110] \"Considering that you said there would be a great difficulty nine years later and still killed the heir, it cannot be considered a normal emotional state of mind.\" [1111] Shima muttered as he walked down the stairs of the windsee tower. [1112] Walking ahead of him, Yuying answered sharply without turning her head, \"Maybe he really thought Spring Vegetable's abilities were not good, just like the threat letter wrote? Or, on the contrary, you can think about it too.\" [1113] \"The opposite?\" [1114] \"The murderer doesn't believe in this either, right? After all, the great disaster won't really happen, so whether Spring Vegetable or Summer Vegetable is the heir, it doesn't matter. If nothing happens in nine years, we can promote it as Summer Vegetable, no, as the power of Iwakura-san that suppressed it. As long as the great disaster is resolved, Iwakura-san will gain even stronger trust and power.\" [1115] \"That's right, but, can it be done smoothly? If nothing happens, people's suspicious gazes will be directed towards the inheritance itself, won't they? And saying that the air raid fifty years ago was just a coincidence. Moreover, Japan today is not the same as during the war, it's actually close to being non-religious.\" [1116] Jinma, of course, did not believe that the disaster would happen. Even if he was born and raised in this village, he would have been half-skeptical, just like Iwakura. Many young people in the village also have a similar feeling. If the disaster is small, won't the feeling of \"it's just as expected\" become stronger? [1117] \"Even if Chunmen girl inherited Fujimoto's mantle, this situation would still occur. The main reason is that Fujimoto's faith is almost at its limit in this era. The apocalyptic thought itself may have the charm of attracting believers, but the problem is that it has become outdated. I wonder how the village will be ten years later. On the contrary, I don't want to know. Anyway, Sumen girl will be very hard-pressed.\" [1118]