uld no longer be available. Of course, I know this is just an unnecessary speculation.\" [3245] \"The revival of the dragon? I never thought of that, and I don't know if there are any records about it. There have been many stories about curses causing disasters, but no one has ever thought of the dragon's revival. Moreover, after that incident, the earthworks were sealed, as we thought that further investigation into the teachings would only lead to unnecessary confusion and disasters. Of course, it was also because we believed that the motive was the 'bloodline theory' mentioned by your mother.\" [3246] \"So, no one is doing any research now? \" [3247] Oyim showed some surprise, and immediately asked, \"Is that so? \" [3248] \"Yes, only the documents and rituals that have been passed down are inherited, and this is what the Dragon's Head Master strongly requested. \" [3249] Zun-one resumed calling Shamanka \"Dragon's Head Master\". Shamanka took up the conversation: \"I believe that the things that have been passed down have their own value in being passed down, and the things that haven't been passed down have their own reasons for not being passed down. My father and Zun-one-san also agree with my opinion. \" [3250] Unlike the previous maternal words of losing a daughter, Shamanka's solemn speech is quite fitting for her identity. [3251] \"I know that the trend of disregarding Shiroki has spread throughout the village. Given the circumstances, it is understandable. Rather than becoming more and more estranged from the villagers by clinging to the doctrine, I would rather become a Shiroki who is trusted for a moment in the present. For example, to be able to casually converse with ordinary villagers in the future. Of course, there are still many conservative people, and it's not easy to achieve, but I have been thinking that it would be nice if the Shiroki doctrine could be expanded even further.\" [3252] Jirou thought this idea was reminiscent of Shiroki's style.